WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.132 --> 00:00:07.935
Do you like apples?

2
00:00:08.495 --> 00:00:12.877
If so, you have a very special man to thank─ or so legend would have you believe.

3
00:00:13.657 --> 00:00:20.980
In 1774 when America was about to become a new nation, a man named John Chapman was born─a

4
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.681
man who would change the face of the American frontier.

5
00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:29.604
Not much is known about his childhood or his family.

6
00:00:30.132 --> 00:00:34.993
However, history books do tell us that he went to school and became very skilled with plants.

7
00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:40.575
There was one plant, however, that John preferred above all other plants: the apple tree.

8
00:00:42.055 --> 00:00:46.757
To him, the apple tree was a sign of hope─the way it blossomed in the spring and produced

9
00:00:46.837 --> 00:00:50.438
enough apples in the fall to feed a person for a full year.

10
00:00:51.098 --> 00:00:55.839
He was a Christian man and was interested in sharing his apple trees and his love for God.

11
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.457
Since he had no wife or children, he decided to leave his home in Massachusetts and travel west

12
00:01:01.477 --> 00:01:02.518
to plant apple trees.

13
00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:10.121
Like many settlers, John had heard rumors that the soil out West was dark and rich and ready

14
00:01:10.161 --> 00:01:11.061
for a good planting.

15
00:01:11.982 --> 00:01:14.342
This was all he needed to know to pack his bag.

16
00:01:15.123 --> 00:01:19.464
In his small bag, he carried a blanket, some apple seeds, his Bible, and a pot.

17
00:01:19.925 --> 00:01:22.366
It wasn't much, but it was all he needed to survive.

18
00:01:23.926 --> 00:01:29.473
John moved from town to town, from plain to plain, from wilderness to wilderness, planting apple seeds.

19
00:01:30.113 --> 00:01:34.175
First, he would look for ground that was near water so the seeds would have something to drink.

20
00:01:34.835 --> 00:01:39.497
Then he would pull out the weeds or tall grasses from the soil, move large rocks and small

21
00:01:39.537 --> 00:01:44.019
stones, and then plow the field so the seedlings could plant their roots.

22
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.102
After the seedlings had broken through the soil, John would build a fence around them to keep animals

23
00:01:51.142 --> 00:01:52.363
from eating the baby trees.

24
00:01:53.043 --> 00:01:55.364
In time the trees would become tall and strong.

25
00:01:55.988 --> 00:01:58.811
Then John would sell the trees to settlers as they moved west.

26
00:01:59.411 --> 00:02:02.514
The settlers would plant and eat from the trees at their new homes.

27
00:02:04.776 --> 00:02:09.960
After all of the trees were sold, John would move farther west himself and begin the plantings again.

28
00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:13.163
Many settlers paid him a penny or two for each tree.

29
00:02:13.723 --> 00:02:17.527
However, some settlers didn't have any money and could only promise to pay him later.

30
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:22.991
Other settlers were moving on and couldn't make that promise, so they paid John with used clothing or

31
00:02:23.051 --> 00:02:23.612
free meals.

32
00:02:25.236 --> 00:02:27.857
John Chapman was loved by all the settlers who knew him.

33
00:02:28.457 --> 00:02:35.581
Wherever he traveled, people spoke about the Apple Tree Man or, as he is popularly known, Johnny Appleseed.

34
00:02:36.101 --> 00:02:40.663
People loved him because he was a kind and gentle man, and because he was generous with what

35
00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:41.523
little he had.

36
00:02:43.304 --> 00:02:49.566
However, as people continued to travel west, stories about Johnny Appleseed sprouted up everywhere.

37
00:02:50.107 --> 00:02:54.296
Some said that he used to talk to animals while he worked, and that he was such a

38
00:02:54.396 --> 00:02:56.757
gentle man the animals actually listened.

39
00:02:58.918 --> 00:03:04.499
In one story, a snowstorm took Johnny Appleseed by surprise, and he had no warm place to sleep.

40
00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:09.840
So he crawled into a hollow log with a mother bear and her cubs and spent the night,

41
00:03:10.161 --> 00:03:11.301
or so the story goes.

42
00:03:13.321 --> 00:03:18.423
Another story said that because Johnny Appleseed didn't wear any shoes, he had very tough feet.

43
00:03:18.943 --> 00:03:23.440
In fact, his feet were so tough that one afternoon, while he was working in the field, a

44
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.322
rattlesnake tried to bite him but couldn't get through his skin!

45
00:03:28.684 --> 00:03:32.866
There is no historical record to prove that any of these Johnny Appleseed stories are true.

46
00:03:33.547 --> 00:03:36.549
Still, all accounts do agree about John Chapman's character.

47
00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:40.611
He was a man who was kind to the Indians and sincere in his Christian faith.

48
00:03:41.492 --> 00:03:46.675
He loved God, and would happily tell anyone about God as he sold them apple trees or gave

49
00:03:46.715 --> 00:03:47.696
them some apples to eat.

50
00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.762
John faithfully planted apple trees all across the wilderness of America, which later became the states of Indiana,

51
00:03:56.282 --> 00:03:58.322
Michigan, Ohio, and Illinois.

52
00:03:59.283 --> 00:04:03.584
No one knows how many trees he planted, but the count is definitely in the millions.

53
00:04:04.024 --> 00:04:08.645
Despite all the trees he planted, he was a very poor man who lived simply, but he seemed

54
00:04:08.685 --> 00:04:09.585
to like it that way.

55
00:04:12.706 --> 00:04:17.327
He was welcomed at any settler's table for dinner, and would happily play with the children and read

56
00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:19.248
them Bible stories when the meal was finished.

57
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.942
Somewhere around the age of seventy, John Chapman died of the winter plague.

58
00:04:24.662 --> 00:04:28.163
Settlers mourned his death, but his generosity blooms each spring.

59
00:04:29.064 --> 00:04:33.726
As apple trees sprout up across the nation, the legend of Johnny Appleseed lives on.
